// Health bands: the range each result of a default run is expected to stay in.
//
// CI runs `econoplex-sim --years 30 --seeds 8 --quiet --bands <this file>` and
// fails when the mean over the 8 seeds of any metric below leaves its band. The
// names are the columns of the batch table.
//
// The bands are wide on purpose: about three times the spread between seeds
// around the values measured on 2026-11-15 (population 1489 +- 186, starving
// 1.06 +- 0.27 %, food per head 2.66 +- 0.53 days, diversity 23.7 +- 2.0).
// They catch a broken economy, not a small shift. Population moved with meals
// as portions (2026-11-15: 2328 over 8 seeds and 30 years, starving 0.32 %,
// food per head 4.15). Food per head moved with the cost floor on prices
// (2026-11-15: 5.37, starving 0.22 %).
//
// A change that is meant to move a result outside its band (for example the
// food work, which should raise food per head well above 5 days) changes the
// band in the same pull request, and says so in its description.
//
// Food yields (2026-11-19, docs/design/food-yields.md): 8 seeds x 30 years
// give food per head 6.3 (10 steps a day) and 7.0 (300), inside the old band.
// `reserve` is the food the towns hold as the year closes, at the end of
// winter, in months of their people's food. The 8-seed mean is 8.5 (10 steps
// a day) and 8.7 (300); the granary holds 2.5-2.7 months of it, the plan's
// 2-3. Single seeds range from 3.0 to 13.6 months, but the band checks the
// mean, so it is the measured means with 2 months either side.
// Kept trades (2026-11-20, plan Phase 3): the mean falls to 7.0 here and 6.2 on
// CI's Linux, since food workers no longer drift in and out of food work; the
// band keeps 2 months below that.
// Own coin (2026-11-26, coins step 2): a treasury now always pays its farmers,
// so towns no longer stop growing food for want of coin. 8 seeds x 30 years
// give food per head 10.3 to 12.4 and an end-of-winter reserve of 12.3 to
// 13.7 months; both bands open upward to 14 and 16. The richer towns also make
// more kinds of goods (diversity 32.5 on CI) and grow to about 2900 people, so
// those bands open to 40 and 4000. Each content batch adds goods, and with the
// fine goods diversity reached 38 (2026-11-27): its band opens to 50, and to
// 60 with the farm foods (47.5). With
// barrels (ale costs wood now) and blankets more work goes to goods other
// than food: the end-of-winter reserve came to 4.2 months with starving at
// 0.02 %, so its band's floor moves to 3 months.
// Hunting and fishing (2026-11-27): wild food is about 2.5 % of the food made,
// but with it 16 seeds x 30 years give food per head 14.4 +- 4.2 (main 12.3 +-
// 3.0) and a reserve of 15.4 (main 13.7). The gap is within about 1.6 standard
// errors of the seed means, and the old upper edges were only 1.7 and 2.3 above
// main, under one seed spread. Both open upward to 18, about one spread above
// the new means.
// Spoilage (2026-11-27, nutrition step 4): every food keeps its own days, and
// a market holds a fresh food only as long as a tenth of them (bread a day,
// fish a few hours). Food in the market falls to 0.95 per head and the
// end-of-winter reserve, now mostly grain in granaries and salted or dried
// food, to 4.35 months (16 seeds x 30 years; population 2894, starving
// 0.01 %). Food per head's floor moves to 0.4 and the reserve's to 2 months;
// starvation stays the check that the towns are fed.
{
    "pop": (1500, 4000),
    "starve%": (0, 2.5),
    "food/cap": (0.4, 18.0),
    "div": (18, 60),
    "reserve": (2.0, 18.0),
}
